  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "If $\\frac{4}{3}=5vs,$ then $s=$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we multiply both sides of the equation by $3$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} & =5vs \\\\ 3\\cdot \\frac{4}{3} & =3\\cdot 5vs \\\\ 4 & =15vs\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to isolate $s.$ To do this, we divide both sides of the equation by $15v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =15vs \\\\ \\frac{4}{15v} & =\\frac{15vs}{15v} \\\\ \\frac{4}{15v} & =\\frac{\\cancel{15v}s}{\\cancel{15v}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{15v} & =s \\\\ s & =\\frac{4}{15v}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
